# <%= it.scope.slice(1) %>-platform

A React micro-frontend platform made with [mfeflow](https://github.com/mfeflow/mfeflow):
one shell and independently deployable remotes, loaded at runtime through Module Federation.

```
apps/shell      the host: routes, layout, registry loading
apps/<remote>   one micro-frontend each, exposing ./App
registry/envs   which build of each remote every environment serves
mfeflow.json    what the CLI generated; `pnpm mfeflow add` reads and updates it
```

## Run it

```sh
pnpm install
pnpm dev
```

`pnpm dev` starts the shell on http://localhost:4300 and every remote on its own port
(see `registry/envs/dev`). The shell reads that dev registry and renders the remotes.

`pnpm mfeflow dev` does the same with a prefixed, coloured log per app, and it follows the project:
add a remote with `pnpm mfeflow add remote checkout` and it starts, and the shell lists it, without a restart.
Use a remote you already run elsewhere with `pnpm mfeflow dev --remote checkout=http://localhost:3001`,
or run a few apps with `--only shell,catalog`. Ctrl+C stops everything.

## Change it

```sh
pnpm mfeflow add remote checkout     # a new remote, its registry files and its shell route
pnpm mfeflow add <feature>           # see `pnpm mfeflow features`
```

## Check it

```sh
pnpm doctor              # singletons, compatibility, config, drift; `pnpm doctor --fix` repairs the safe ones
pnpm validate            # mfeflow.json, every mfeflow.app.json and every registry entry
pnpm check-compat        # each remote's shared ranges against its shell
pnpm budget              # after `pnpm build`: first-load JS per shell and remote
```

Each is a root script for the `mfeflow` CLI in your devDependencies (`pnpm mfeflow <command>` runs it directly). All of them take `--json` and exit non-zero on errors, so they can run in CI. `pnpm budget` checks each remote's entry
against the 10 KB a remote may add; set `budget.baselineKb` in `mfeflow.json` (your pages built as one app) to check
the shell against baseline + 32 KB too.

## Deploy it

```sh
pnpm build
pnpm mfeflow deploy catalog --env staging --host file:./site --base-url https://cdn.example.com
pnpm mfeflow promote catalog --from staging --to prod --host file:./site
pnpm mfeflow rollback catalog --env prod --host file:./site
pnpm mfeflow status --host file:./site
```

`deploy` uploads a remote's `dist` and points an environment at it, `rollback` points it back without a rebuild, and
`status` shows what is served. Put `host` and `baseUrl` under `registry` in `mfeflow.json` to drop the flags, or use
`s3://<bucket>` with the `MFEFLOW_S3_*` and `AWS_*` variables. There are no root scripts for these, because the package manager has a deploy command of its own, so
run them through `pnpm mfeflow`.

## Before you deploy

`registry/envs/staging` and `registry/envs/prod` point at `example.com`. Replace the hosts
with where you serve each remote (the shell expects `/mfe/<name>/` on its own origin).
The shell loads the registry from `/registry/index.json`, so serve one per environment.
